  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nRAIN AND RAINSHOWERS RETURN FOR THE START OF THE WEEKEND:\n\nBroad southwesterly flow continues across the area and as a \nshortwave trough moves through the region tomorrow, models \ncontinue to highlight some showers and storms across the CWA. \nThis southwesterly flow will keep warm temperatures in place so \nmost of this precipitation will fall as rain though a few inches\ncan't be ruled out over the highest terrain of the San Juans. \nIf stronger thunderstorms do develop, brief heavy rain may lead\nto some very isolated flooding issues but the bigger concern \nwill be the push of moisture on Sunday.\n\nWETTER, UNSETTLED, AND SNOWY SUNDAY AND MONDAY:\n\nA robust Atmospheric River arrives on Sunday bringing well \nabove normal amounts of moisture for this time of year across\nthe desert southwest. Guidance continues to show precip moving \ninto the Four Corners Sunday morning with heavier precip \nquickly moving in about 3 hours later as the precip spreads\nnortheastward. This on again/off again moderate to heavier \nprecip will continue through early Monday morning before \ntapering off to more showery type precip. The recent rainfall \nand flooding from last week is still a concern as we receive \nanywhere from 2 inches for the Southern Valleys and 3 to close \nto 4 inches of liquid precip over the San Juans. Flooding \nremains a concern so the Flood Watch remains in place. More in \nthe Hydrology section below.\n\nThe NBM and other models have also been showing temperatures\ncold enough for some snow accumulation, especially over the San\nJuans. Despite snow levels increasing through the day Sunday,\nhad enough confidence to issue a Winter Storm Watch for the San\nJuans for elevations above 10K feet for over a foot of snow and\nadvisories for portions of the central mountains for 5 to 10 \ninches of snow, also above 10,000 feet. There is still some\nuncertainty in the forecast though confidence does continue to\nincrease. Either way, please pay attention to the latest\nforecasts if you'll be travelling this weekend, especially over \nthe higher terrain or the Four Corners region.",
